<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Alpha-out curve for the CLOSE-exit of the platform's custom cross-Activity
     back animation.

     The Shell evaluates this resource with Animation#getTransformationAt(progress),
     which *does* apply the child interpolator (Animation.java: interpolatedTime =
     mInterpolator.getInterpolation(normalizedTime)) but never renormalises by the
     child duration. An interpolator is therefore the only supported way to make the
     closing page fade faster than the 450 ms geometry clock. Shortening the alpha
     child duration would change nothing on the Shell path and would desynchronise
     the plain finish() window transition, which is driven by the same resource.

     Output y is the alpha interpolation fraction, so alpha = 1 - y. The curve
     reaches y = 1 at x = 0.26 and stays there, i.e. the closing page is fully gone
     after ~26% of the post-commit clock (~117 ms of 450 ms). That mirrors
     DefaultCrossActivityBackAnimation's reference alpha max(1 - progress * 5, 0),
     which is fully transparent at 20%, while keeping a short tail instead of a hard
     cutoff at exactly 20%.

     PathInterpolator contract (PathInterpolator#initPath): the path must start at
     (0,0), end at (1,1), and x must be monotonic. The cubic keeps x increasing
     (0.09 -> 0.18 -> 0.26) and y increasing, and the final horizontal segment keeps
     x increasing with y pinned at 1. Endpoints are exact so the shell's unclamped
     0..1 progress evaluates to alpha 1 at 0 and 0 at 1. -->
<pathInterpolator xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:pathData="M0,0 C0.09,0.36 0.18,0.92 0.26,1 C0.5,1 0.75,1 1,1" />
